WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.433 --> 00:00:12.294
how big its feet are!"

2
00:00:12.875 --> 00:00:13.615
Eric pointed.

3
00:00:13.635 --> 00:00:16.338
"How did they find all the bones?"

4
00:00:16.918 --> 00:00:18.179
Dana raised her hand.

5
00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:22.363
All the kids in the class were looking at a dinosaur exhibit.

6
00:00:22.383 --> 00:00:24.765
"Do you want to know a secret?"

7
00:00:25.205 --> 00:00:28.248
Miss Bennett, the museum guide, said in a low voice.

8
00:00:29.192 --> 00:00:32.333
"Those are not real dinosaur bones!"

9
00:00:57.736 --> 00:00:58.697
"But but but," Eric stuttered.

10
00:00:58.717 --> 00:00:59.817
"So dinosaurs never existed?"

11
00:00:59.837 --> 00:01:00.978
Carol asked timidly.

12
00:01:00.998 --> 00:01:02.018
"Of course they existed!"

13
00:01:02.078 --> 00:01:02.898
Miss Bennett laughed.

14
00:01:02.918 --> 00:01:04.099
"But those bones are not the original bones.

15
00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:05.520
Follow me and I will explain."

16
00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:08.041
The class followed her into another room.

17
00:01:08.061 --> 00:01:09.461
"First, who knows where those bones came from?"

18
00:01:09.481 --> 00:01:09.881
Miss Bennett asked.

19
00:01:09.901 --> 00:01:10.382
"From dead dinosaurs?"

20
00:01:10.402 --> 00:01:10.682
Eric answered.

21
00:01:10.702 --> 00:01:12.443
"They come from fossils!"

22
00:01:12.983 --> 00:01:13.683
Carol said.

23
00:01:13.703 --> 00:01:15.724
"You are both right.

24
00:01:16.564 --> 00:01:19.646
The dinosaur's dead bones turned into fossils."

25
00:01:22.347 --> 00:01:24.468
Miss Bennett switched on a projector.

26
00:01:25.116 --> 00:01:27.638
She pointed at the dinosaur on the screen.

27
00:01:27.658 --> 00:01:29.779
"Look at this dinosaur.

28
00:01:30.499 --> 00:01:36.323
Usually, when a dinosaur dies, it decays or another animal eats it.

29
00:01:37.123 --> 00:01:41.606
But sometimes the dinosaur is buried before it gets destroyed.

30
00:01:45.828 --> 00:01:49.310
Slowly the flesh and skin of the dinosaur rot away.

31
00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:51.491
Only the bones are left.

32
00:01:52.192 --> 00:01:56.211
Then more and more mud and sand cover the bones.

33
00:01:57.112 --> 00:02:02.616
The mud and sand, which are called sediment, press down on the bones.

34
00:02:03.357 --> 00:02:05.098
The sediment has water in it.

35
00:02:05.839 --> 00:02:07.340
The water has minerals.

36
00:02:11.483 --> 00:02:14.246
The minerals soak into the dinosaur bones.

37
00:02:15.186 --> 00:02:19.170
Slowly the bone material is replaced by the minerals.

38
00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:21.792
The bones become hard rock.

39
00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:24.261
Now they are fossils.

40
00:02:28.383 --> 00:02:31.584
This process takes a very long time.

41
00:02:32.345 --> 00:02:37.667
It takes thousands and thousands of years for the sediment to form heavy layers.

42
00:02:38.528 --> 00:02:43.210
These heavy layers turn into sedimentary rock around the fossils.

43
00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:46.952
So most fossils are found in this rock."

44
00:02:50.252 --> 00:02:54.693
When the slide show was over, Miss Bennett gave everyone a slab of clay.

45
00:02:54.713 --> 00:03:00.595
"Let's try a simple experiment," she said as she held up her hand.

46
00:03:00.615 --> 00:03:03.416
"Place your hand on the clay.

47
00:03:03.996 --> 00:03:08.477
Pretend that your hand is a dead dinosaur covered with sand and mud.

48
00:03:10.898 --> 00:03:16.940
Press down on your hand and imagine it is being covered by more layers of sand and mud.

49
00:03:17.884 --> 00:03:23.227
Imagine the sediment keeps pressing for thousands, even millions of years!"

50
00:03:23.247 --> 00:03:27.189
"Ouch! My hand hurts, Miss Bennett!"

51
00:03:27.649 --> 00:03:28.270
Eric said.

52
00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:32.492
Everyone laughed, and Miss Bennett continued her explanation.

53
00:03:37.155 --> 00:03:41.197
took many years for the dinosaur bones to become fossils.

54
00:03:41.917 --> 00:03:46.900
Finally scientists dug the fossils out of the sedimentary rock.

55
00:03:47.776 --> 00:03:50.899
They had to be very careful not to break the rock.

56
00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:55.302
After they cleaned the rock, what did they find?"

57
00:03:55.963 --> 00:03:56.823
asked Miss Bennett.

58
00:03:56.843 --> 00:03:59.085
"A fossil!"

59
00:03:59.606 --> 00:04:00.626
everybody answered.

60
00:04:01.547 --> 00:04:03.849
They all held up their slabs of clay.

61
00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:06.851
On each one was a handprint.

62
00:04:07.572 --> 00:04:13.096
"Your handprint is not your actual hand," said Miss Bennett.

63
00:04:13.116 --> 00:04:16.839
"But we can still see that it was a hand.

64
00:04:18.152 --> 00:04:23.017
In the same way, fossils are not the actual bones of a dinosaur."

65
00:04:23.037 --> 00:04:29.943
"Is that why you said that the dinosaur bones we saw are not real?"

66
00:04:30.403 --> 00:04:31.324
called out Eric.

67
00:04:32.265 --> 00:04:34.247
"Yes, you are right."

68
00:04:34.867 --> 00:04:35.868
Miss Bennett nodded.

69
00:04:35.888 --> 00:04:44.136
"Remember, the original bones are replaced by rocklike minerals," Miss Bennett explained.

70
00:04:45.028 --> 00:04:52.070
"So a fossil is just a model of the original bone, like your handprint.

71
00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:56.491
And the dinosaur bones in the exhibit are models of the fossils."

72
00:04:56.511 --> 00:05:00.872
"Thank you, Miss Bennett," the children said together.

73
00:05:00.892 --> 00:05:03.773
"We learned a lot today."

74
00:05:03.793 --> 00:05:06.254
"Come back anytime!"

75
00:05:06.834 --> 00:05:07.894
Miss Bennett waved.

76
00:05:08.694 --> 00:05:12.876
"There is still more to learn about the world of fossils."
